    <desc>Show several terminals side by side or one above the other in one tab.</desc>
  </info>

  <title>Split panes</title>

<p>A tab can be split into several terminals, called panes, side by side or
  one above the other. A new pane starts in the working folder of the active
  one.</p>

  <section id="split">
    <title>Split a tab</title>
    <p>Press <keyseq><key>Shift</key><key>Ctrl</key><key>R</key></keyseq> to open a new pane on the right of the
    active one, or <keyseq><key>Shift</key><key>Ctrl</key><key>D</key></keyseq> to open it below. These are also
    <gui style="menuitem">Split Right</gui> and
    <gui style="menuitem">Split Down</gui> in the main menu and in the context
    menu of a terminal.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="use">
    <title>Move between panes and resize them</title>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><keyseq><key>Alt</key><key>Arrow keys</key></keyseq></title>
        <p>Move the focus to the neighboring pane in that direction. You can
        also click a pane.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><keyseq><key>Alt</key><key>Shift</key><key>Arrow keys</key></keyseq></title>
        <p>Move the divider of the active pane. You can also drag the
        divider.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><keyseq><key>Shift</key><key>Ctrl</key><key>Z</key></keyseq></title>
        <p>Show only the active pane, and all panes again.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><keyseq><key>Shift</key><key>Ctrl</key><key>X</key></keyseq></title>
        <p>Close the active pane.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
    <p>These shortcuts only take effect while a tab is split, so programs in
    a single terminal still get them.</p>
    <p>The context menu of a split tab can also make all panes the same size,
    and swap the active pane with a neighboring one.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="move">
    <title>Move panes</title>
    <p>Right-click a pane and select <gui style="menuitem">Move Pane</gui> to
    move it, with what runs in it, to a new tab or a new window.</p>
    <p>You can also drag a pane by its title, once you have turned on
    <link xref="#look">pane titles</link>. Drop it onto the side of another
    pane to put it there, onto the middle of a pane of the same tab to swap
    the two, onto the tabs to make it a tab of its own, or out of the window
    to give it a new window. A tab that you drag onto a pane becomes panes
    there.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="look">
    <title>Pane titles and dimming</title>
    <p>The panes that you are not typing in are dimmed. To change this, and
    to show the title of each pane above it:</p>
    <steps>
<item><p>Press the menu button in the top-right corner of the window and select <gui style="menuitem">Preferences</gui>.</p></item>
<item><p>In the <gui>Appearance</gui> group, turn <gui>Dim Inactive Panes</gui> and <gui>Show Pane Titles</gui> on or off.</p></item>
</steps>
  </section>

